{
  "passage": "Long ago, before money was used, people exchanged goods directly for other goods. This is called the barter system. Imagine a shoemaker who wants to sell shoes and buy wheat. In a barter system, he must find a farmer who not only has wheat to sell but also wants to buy shoes at the same time. This is called the double coincidence of wants: what one person wishes to sell is exactly what the other wishes to buy. Finding such a person is often very difficult. Money solves this problem. The shoemaker can sell his shoes to anyone for money, and then use that money to buy wheat from any farmer. Because money acts as a go-between in exchanges, it is called a medium of exchange. Over history, many things have been used as money, including grains, cattle, shells, and coins of gold, silver and copper. Today, we use paper notes and coins issued by the Reserve Bank of India on behalf of the government. No one in India can refuse to accept the rupee in payment, because the law makes it the official currency. People also keep money in banks as deposits. Banks keep only a small part of these deposits as cash, about fifteen per cent, and use the rest to give loans to people who need them, charging interest.",
